# Alice's Adventures in Wonderland read whole over six sittings in the observatory's term. The text is
# the volume alice-in-wonderland; every evidence question is proved against its lines.
lesson reading-alice-in-wonderland v=1 format=book grade=5 unit=4 subject=reading book=alice-in-wonderland levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Alice's Adventures in Wonderland"
  goal "Read Alice's Adventures in Wonderland from start to end, and prove what you say about it with lines from the book."

  sitting chapters=[1, 2] {
    show reading.alice-line k=1 easy-k=0
    show reading.alice-line k=2 hard-k=3
    show reading.alice-sum k=0
    level medium hard {
      show reading.alice-word k=0
    }
  }
  sitting chapters=[3, 4] {
    show reading.alice-line k=5 easy-k=4
    show reading.alice-line k=6 hard-k=7
    show reading.alice-order k=0
    show reading.alice-why k=0
  }
  sitting chapters=[5, 6] {
    show reading.alice-line k=9 easy-k=8
    show reading.alice-line k=10 hard-k=11
    level medium hard {
      show reading.alice-who k=0
    }
    show reading.alice-word k=1
  }
  sitting chapters=[7, 8] {
    show reading.alice-line k=13 easy-k=12
    show reading.alice-line k=14 hard-k=15
    show reading.alice-sum k=1
    level medium hard {
      show reading.alice-who k=1
    }
  }
  sitting chapters=[9, 10] {
    show reading.alice-line k=17 easy-k=16
    show reading.alice-line k=18 hard-k=19
    level medium hard {
      show reading.alice-word k=2
    }
    show reading.alice-why k=1
  }
  sitting chapters=[11, 12] {
    show reading.alice-line k=21 easy-k=20
    show reading.alice-line k=22 hard-k=23
    level medium hard {
      show reading.alice-who k=2
    }
    level hard {
      show reading.alice-order k=1
    }
    show reading.alice-verdict
    show reading.alice-changed
    show reading.alice-review
  }

  grown-ups """
    The book is read over six sittings across the observatory's term, one a day, two chapters a
    sitting, the chapters first and the questions after. Each sitting is about 4,000 to 4,800 words,
    20 to 25 minutes for a child of ten to twelve at about 200 words a minute, which leaves time to
    read the poems aloud: Carroll's verses (How doth the little crocodile, You are old, Father William,
    the Lobster Quadrille) are parodies of poems Victorian children learnt, and they sound best said.
    Every sitting has two questions answered with a line number, and the questions never use the
    line's own words, so a child who skims will not find them; the idea to watch for is an answer
    that remembers the story but cannot point to the words that prove it, so after each answer ask
    "which words show it?" On paper the family reads any edition, and the grown-ups' sheet gives the
    words of each line beside its number, so mark by the words. In chapter 10 our text keeps two
    short bracketed passages that later editions of the poems added; they are marked as such and no
    question depends on them. If the jokes on words puzzle the child (Tortoise because he taught us,
    Reeling and Writhing), say them aloud together, since most of them are heard rather than seen. The
    questions marked by a grown-up (who Alice is when she keeps changing size, the Duchess's morals,
    how Alice changes, and the review) have a list of what to notice; talk about them before ticking.
    Off the page, look at the night sky from a window and find the brightest star, then read the
    last page again, where Alice's sister imagines her grown up and telling the story to children of
    her own. The lesson meets China's 2022 standard for grades five and six (第三学段, 阅读与鉴赏 6),
    「阅读整本书，把握文本的主要内容」, reading a whole book and grasping its main content; the Common Core
    names this book among its exemplar texts for these grades.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level one evidence question in each sitting asks for a line whose words are close to the question's, and the who-said questions and two of the word questions are left out."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level one evidence question in each sitting asks for what a line shows without saying it, such as the first sign the baby is a pig, and the last sitting adds putting the trial's events in order."
  }
}
